A||||2||helpme||1984 fiero will not start||||1||
Z||000000||helpme||02-12-2000||02:23 PM||||i have an 1984 fiero, it will not stay running. i have poured gas down the carb, it will run just until the gas i poured down the carb is gone. does anyone have an idea on what i should check next. thanks for the input. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sage||02-12-2000||03:09 PM||||Do you hear the fuel pump running when you turn the key on? Check your fuses.  Do you have a haynes manual or any manual with trouble shooting section?  If it runs when you pour fuel in then stops, it is not getting fuel from the tank, you have to determine why. Check your fuses first.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||02-12-2000||06:59 PM||||It's not really a carb. It is a throttle body fuel injection unit.  There are a number of reasons why it may not be injecting fuel into the engine.  Of course, a non-functioning pump could be the culprit. Turn the ignition key to "on" and listen for the sound of the electric pump. It should run for 2 or 3 seconds and shut itself off.  If the pump is running and there is gas in the tank then there should be fuel pressure at the throttle body unit.  You can carefully loosen the fuel line connector at the filter and see if there is any pressure. BE CAREFUL--use a rag or something so fuel doesn't spray and of course no flames. If you have fuel pressure, then maybe the injector is not working for some reason.  You can test the injector circuit with an injector test light (sometimes called a "noid light") that is available from most auto parts stores.  Take the connector off of the injector and plug the test light into it and have someone attempt to start the engine.  The light should flash if the engine control computer and wiring are working.  If the light does flash, you may have a bad injector.  If it doesnt' flash, there is a problem either with wiring or the engine control computer.  Good Luck!||||reg||1
